---
/**
 * foodcraft / AboutBN — "Panday Coffee Works" (BN), the ABOUT section.
 *
 * ROLE: the roastery describing itself, set as a page of a technical document.
 * The design draws the spine's rail ("01 / About") and then splits the content
 * cell in two EQUAL halves — the story on the LEFT behind a 1px hairline, a
 * 440px photograph on the flat --well ground on the RIGHT — and rules a
 * three-across SPEC LEDGER of label/value facts underneath both of them, edge
 * to edge. There is no radius anywhere and no gap anywhere: every division in
 * this section is a BORDER, which is the whole point of the template.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="ABOUT" in
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html":
 *   section     border-bottom 1px #17181A, grid minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)
 *               → the wrapper's .sec, whose --rail-w IS that 52px
 *   rail        border-right 1px #C8C7C2, flex end/centre, padding 20px 0,
 *               label vertical-rl + rotate(180deg), mono 10px / .3em / #A8351B
 *               → the wrapper's .rail + .rail-t, used and NOT rebuilt
 *   split       grid minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr), NO gap
 *   copy cell   padding 56px 40px, border-right 1px #C8C7C2
 *   headline    800 clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) / line-height 1 / -.03em / uppercase
 *               / margin 0 0 .34em / #17181A
 *   paragraphs  16.5px / 1.82 / #3C3E42, margin 0 0 1em, the LAST one 0
 *   photo cell  position relative, min-height 440px, ground #DEDCD6
 *   ledger      grid repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), border-top 1px #C8C7C2
 *   ledger cell padding 16px 24px, border-right AND border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2,
 *               flex, space-between, gap 12px, align-items baseline
 *   label       mono 10px / .14em / uppercase / #5A5C60
 *   value       600 14.5px #17181A
 * The photograph stays on the RIGHT, the ledger keeps all three of its tracks
 * above 900px, and no division has been converted from a border into a gap to
 * make an editor mechanism easier. That last habit is what would turn this
 * template back into the soft one it deliberately is not.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S OWN. An audit had to rename a whole set of
 * classes a previous template copied verbatim from its sibling, so nothing here
 * reuses AboutBM's `.tah-about-*` or AboutBL's `.story-*`: every scoped class is
 * prefixed `.pcw-ab-` for Panday Coffee Works. Only the HOUSE STYLE crossed
 * over from AboutBM — this doc block's shape, the tolerant reads, the
 * guard-outside-the-tag discipline.
 *
 * R1 · NO LITERAL HEX. Every hex the design writes in this element already has
 * a name in PageBN and is used only through it:
 *   #17181A --ink · #3C3E42 --ink-2 · #5A5C60 --muted · #C8C7C2 --line
 *   #DEDCD6 --well · #A8351B --accent (the rail label, via .rail-t) · 0 --r
 * Note the two rule weights are NOT interchangeable here: every hairline INSIDE
 * this section is the quiet --line, while the rule that closes the section is
 * the full-strength --rule-strong, and that one is drawn by .sec and never
 * restated below. --muted is remapped by the engine to palette.inkSoft, so the
 * ledger label follows the scheme's soft ink rather than a value this file
 * pins.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `about` group, so each data-field below is a hook a real sidebar
 * input owns):
 *   about.image             (data-image-field) — the right-hand photograph
 *   about.headline          — the h2, set:html so an owner's own <em> lands
 *                             where THEY put it; the design's second voice is a
 *                             COLOUR change (PageBN's `:where(.h2 em)` → rust),
 *                             never an italic — Archivo has none worth using
 *   about.lead              — the FIRST paragraph. Rendered on purpose: the
 *                             sidebar's "Lead paragraph" box writes about.lead,
 *                             and a schema field no component renders is the
 *                             mirror image of a dead hook and just as broken.
 *   about.paragraphs.${i}   — the remaining paragraphs (set:html)
 *   about.specs.${i}.label  — the ledger cell's left span
 *   about.specs.${i}.value  — the ledger cell's right span
 * about.signature is READ once — as the photograph's accessible name — and is
 * never printed. See R9.
 *
 * R2 · NOTHING VISIBLE IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. Not one word. The design's demo
 * copy ("A workshop / that happens / to serve coffee", the two body paragraphs,
 * the six sample spec rows) is the DESIGNER filling their own template, and the
 * image slot's "Drop the roaster / bar" is an editor affordance, not page copy —
 * none of it ships. A roastery that wrote no spec rows advertises none.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT and
 * not merely its text: no headline over nothing, no empty paragraph spending a
 * margin, no empty plate, and — because each ledger cell carries its OWN two
 * hairlines — no rule drawn around a row that is not there.
 * NO STAR ROW exists in this section: the design's ABOUT carries no rating and
 * the schema exposes no numeric rating on about.*, so there is nothing here that
 * could print five stars for a customer who gave no number.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never invented):
 *   · the rail label "01 / About". It is DECORATIVE and DERIVED — an index of
 *     render position, not content: About is the 4th component PageBN renders
 *     but the 2nd to carry a rail (Trust is full-bleed), and the design numbers
 *     the RAILS, giving Hero 00 and About 01. It is never an editable hook and
 *     never stored. The design's own word for this section is "The works",
 *     which is the designer describing THEIR coffee shop; the index word here
 *     is the neutral one this family uses, because a dentist's page must not
 *     announce a roastery's joke.
 *   · paraRows / specRows drop blank entries but carry the ORIGINAL array index
 *     into data-field (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row
 *     3. Nothing in this section is numbered on screen, so no display index is
 *     needed — the rail label is the section's index, not a row's.
 *   · a spec row survives on EITHER cell: a label the owner has typed but not
 *     yet answered is still their line, and only a wholly empty row is dropped.
 *   · about.paragraphs / about.specs are read tolerantly (array or bare string;
 *     objects or bare strings inside). Draft content has arrived as both shapes
 *     before, and a `.map is not a function` costs the whole page, not one row.
 *   · the split drops to ONE track whenever only one side has content, so a
 *     missing photograph never leaves a 440px hole beside the copy — the common
 *     case, since derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image and a first build
 *     is therefore copy-only. In that state the copy cell also drops its
 *     border-right, because a hairline down the middle of a half-empty sheet is
 *     a division between something and nothing. When NEITHER side has content —
 *     spec rows and nothing else, the only way to reach this section without a
 *     lead, a paragraph or a photograph — the split is not emitted at all and
 *     the ledger drops its opening border-top, so the section cannot start with
 *     a hairline dividing the ledger from nothing.
 *   · the image url has its apostrophes percent-escaped before it goes into
 *     url('…'), so an upload named "panday's roaster.jpg" cannot close the quote
 *     early and take the whole declaration down with it.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5): the headline is deliberately NOT counted.
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts writes about.headline on every named site (and
 * about.tag on every site at all), so a gate counting either would be
 * always-true and would ship a heading over an empty section. The gate counts
 * BODY content only: the lead, the surviving paragraphs, the surviving spec
 * rows, the photograph.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of these reads as an oversight:
 *   · about.tag. The design's ABOUT draws NO eyebrow — the rail label is what
 *     names this section on the sheet, and a .kick above the headline would be
 *     an element the design does not have. This is a real cost and is stated
 *     rather than hidden: the sidebar's "Eyebrow tag" input under About has no
 *     visible effect on THIS template. Reproducing the design was judged the
 *     stronger duty; if that trade is rejected, the eyebrow belongs above the
 *     h2 as a .kick bound to about.tag and nothing else here changes.
 *   · about.role and about.signature as VISIBLE text. They belong to a host
 *     identity row, which is a different design; this ABOUT draws a story, a
 *     photograph and a ledger, and no person.
 *   · a .cap pill on the plate. The design's about photograph carries no caption
 *     and no schema field would let an owner write one, so a caption here could
 *     only be a literal this file invented.
 *   · .sec-body. It is the wrapper's PADDED content cell, and this section's
 *     content cell is deliberately unpadded: the design pads the copy half
 *     (56px 40px) and the ledger cells (16px 24px) individually so that the
 *     hairlines between them can run edge to edge. Padding the outer cell as
 *     well would inset every rule and break the sheet. .sec, .rail and .rail-t
 *     ARE used, unmodified — the spine is not rebuilt here.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR HELPER (R7). This section renders no href of any kind —
 *     the design's ABOUT has no button, no link and no action — so there is no
 *     data-href-field to remap, and a helper that is never called is dead code
 *     that later drifts out of step with the wrapper. If a link is ever added
 *     here it must arrive WITH the helper copied in from a sibling, and for THIS
 *     page that means PAGE_ANCHORS = menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main
 *     with ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location + contact → visit, services + rooms →
 *     menu, host + why → about, work → gallery, book → main, and off-page hrefs
 *     (tel:, mailto:, http) passing through untouched. The anchor duty this file
 *     DOES own is its id="about" — one of PageBN's six page anchors, and the
 *     target the header nav and the host/why aliases resolve to.
 *   · any button. The design's ABOUT has none, so none of the wrapper's four
 *     (.btn--fill / .btn--ink / .btn--orange / .btn--cream) appears below, and
 *     neither does any engine-pinned name (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta
 *     .cta-band .trust .testi, or `.hero` on a <section>) — R4.
 *
 * A KNOWN RAGGEDNESS, stated rather than papered over: the ledger is a fixed
 * three-track grid and each cell draws its own right and bottom hairline, which
 * is exactly what the design does with its six demo rows. An owner who writes
 * four rows therefore gets a second row of one cell, and that row's rule stops a
 * third of the way across. Filling the gap would mean drawing borders under
 * cells that do not exist — a hairline under a row that is not there, which R2
 * forbids — so the ragged edge is kept.
 *
 * R9 · the plate renders ONLY when there is a real upload, so role="img" is
 * honest by construction. Its accessible name is about.signature — the owner's
 * OWN words (derive-content-defaults seeds it from the business name, so the
 * photograph announces as the business). Never a literal written here. With no
 * signature the photo is HIDDEN from the accessibility tree rather than
 * announced as something we guessed, and that is done with ONE conditional
 * aria-hidden — never a literal aria-hidden="true" sitting beside a conditional
 * one, which is the duplicated-attribute bug earlier components shipped: the
 * literal wins and hides content that should have been announced. The rail
 * label is the section's other a11y decision: it is a decorative index and the
 * one string this file writes, so it is aria-hidden and the <h2> is what
 * announces this section.
 *
 * R3 · one scoped style block, no is:global. Astro's scoped styles cannot reach
 * elements created by set:html, so the owner's own <em> inside the headline is
 * styled by PageBN's global `:where(.h2 em)` rule (rust, font-style normal) and
 * NOT from here. Anything this file ever needed to say about an owner <em>
 * would have to go through :global().
 *
 * R8 · RESPONSIVE, and every clamp traced across both boundaries.
 *   · the split and the ledger both collapse before 900px: split 2 → 1, ledger
 *     3 → 2, and the ledger 2 → 1 at 520px. The copy cell's border-RIGHT becomes
 *     a border-BOTTOM when it stacks, so the sheet stays ruled in one column.
 *   · copy padding is ONE clamp at every width — clamp(28px,4vw,56px)
 *     clamp(20px,3vw,40px) — whose caps ARE the design's 56/40. One expression,
 *     no breakpoint override, so it cannot step up as the viewport narrows.
 *   · the plate is a flat 440px min-height above 900 and clamp(220px,44vw,380px)
 *     below it: at the 900px boundary that clamp yields its 380px cap (44vw =
 *     396), which is BELOW 440, and from there it only falls. A floor sitting
 *     under a cap is the exact failure two audits found; there is none here.
 *   · the headline's size is this file's, not the wrapper's, and that is
 *     deliberate. PageBN's shared curve is base clamp(28px,4.4vw,62px) with a
 *     760 pass of clamp(26px,7.4vw,40px): at 761px the base clamp yields its vw
 *     term, 4.4vw = 33.5px, while one pixel narrower the query yields its 40px
 *     cap (7.4vw = 56.3), so a .h2 that took the shared curve would JUMP from
 *     33.5px UP to 40px as the viewport NARROWS across that boundary. This section's headline is the design's own
 *     clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) declared once, with no query of its own, so it
 *     falls from 50px to its 28px floor (reached at 777.8px) and is flat below
 *     that — monotonic by construction. The shared bug is raised against PageBN,
 *     where all fifteen sections would get the fix, and is not patched here.
 *   · at 360px nothing may push sideways: the headline, both paragraph kinds and
 *     both ledger cells carry overflow-wrap: anywhere, so a hyphenless bean
 *     origin or a pasted URL wraps instead of widening the document. There is no
 *     button in this section, so the full-width `white-space: normal` +
 *     `overflow-wrap: anywhere` pair has nothing here to apply to.
 *
 * CONTRAST (WCAG 2.x, sRGB relative luminance). Every figure below is READ OFF
 * the family's checked BN ratio table for the hexes PageBN declares, not done
 * by hand here — hand arithmetic is what put three earlier audits in this
 * family at odds over the same pair:
 *   headline / ledger value  --ink #17181A on --paper  15.30:1
 *   paragraphs               --ink-2 #3C3E42 on --paper 9.23:1
 *   ledger label             --muted #5A5C60 on --paper 5.77:1
 *   rail label               --accent #A8351B on --paper 5.67:1
 * Everything this section prints is above AA — the tightest pair here is 5.67
 * at 10px, and normal text needs 4.50. PageBN's own header table agrees with
 * every one of these it carries (15.30 · 5.77 · 5.67); the 9.23 pair is this
 * section's alone and the wrapper does not list it.
 * NOTHING IS OUTSTANDING AT THE WRAPPER. PageBN's `.plate .cap` comment reads
 * 15.30:1 for --on-ink on --ink, and that is the table's value: --on-ink is the
 * same #EFEEEA as --paper, so the pair is ink/paper inverted. An earlier note
 * here claimed the wrapper said 14.4:1 there — it does not — and this section
 * renders no .cap, so that pair is not one this file paints.
 * ALL FOUR figures are NATIVE VALUES ONLY. buildOverrideCss rewrites --muted
 * (to palette.inkSoft), --accent and --ink, and — the one that catches every
 * pair at once — the --paper GROUND, each with !important, so a scheme pick
 * repaints at least one end of every pair listed above. Only --ink-2 is
 * invisible to the engine, and it is half of a single pair.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;

const about = content.about || {};

const headline = about.headline || '';
const lead = about.lead || '';
const image = String(about.image || '');
// An apostrophe in an upload's filename would otherwise close the url('') early
// and take the whole background-image declaration down with it.
const imageCss = image.replace(/'/g, '%27');

// Read tolerantly: a draft has arrived with this as a bare string before, and a
// `.map is not a function` costs the whole page rather than one paragraph. The
// only fallback taken is the SAME path as a string — folding in a legacy
// `about.body` is not done here, because the data-field would then point at
// about.paragraphs.0 while the text lived somewhere else, and an input that
// edits a different string than the one under it is a broken hook.
const paragraphSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.paragraphs)
  ? about.paragraphs
  : (typeof about.paragraphs === 'string' && about.paragraphs.trim()
    ? [about.paragraphs]
    : []);
// A blank row in the admin's paragraph list would otherwise render an empty <p>
// that still spends its margin. Drop the blanks, carry the ORIGINAL index (R6).
const paraRows = paragraphSource
  .map((text: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(text ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((row) => row.text.trim().length > 0);

// Same discipline for the ledger. An item may legitimately be an object
// {label,value} (what the schema's newItem creates) or, from an older draft, a
// bare string — treated as a label with no value rather than dropped.
const specSource: any[] = Array.isArray(about.specs) ? about.specs : [];
const specRows = specSource
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    const item = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { label: row, value: '' };
    return {
      label: String(item.label ?? '').trim(),
      value: String(item.value ?? '').trim(),
      i,
    };
  })
  // EITHER cell keeps the row. Only a wholly empty row goes, so no hairline is
  // ever drawn around a row that is not there.
  .filter((row) => row.label.length > 0 || row.value.length > 0);

// The photograph's accessible name — the owner's own words, never a literal.
const photoName = String(about.signature || '').trim();

// Track count, computed here so the published split never keeps an empty 440px
// column standing beside a single populated one.
const hasCopy = Boolean(headline || lead) || paraRows.length > 0;
const hasPhoto = Boolean(image);
// R5 — BODY content only. The headline is excluded: it is derived onto every
// named site, so counting it would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = Boolean(lead || image) || paraRows.length > 0 || specRows.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sec pcw-ab" id="about">
    {/* THE SPINE. .sec, .rail and .rail-t are PageBN's primitives, used as they
        are: the 52px track, the right hairline and the rotated mono index. The
        label is decorative and derived — see DERIVED above — so it is hidden
        from the accessibility tree and the h2 announces this section. */}
    <div class="rail">
      <span class="rail-t" aria-hidden="true">About</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pcw-ab-body">
      {(hasCopy || hasPhoto) && (
      <div class:list={['pcw-ab-split', !(hasCopy && hasPhoto) && 'pcw-ab-split--solo']}>
        {hasCopy && (
          <div class="pcw-ab-copy">
            {headline && (
              <h2 class="h2 pcw-ab-h reveal" data-field="about.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
            )}
            {/* about.lead comes from a plain textarea, so it is bound as TEXT.
                The paragraph LIST below is where an owner writes markup. */}
            {lead && (
              <p class="pcw-ab-p reveal" data-delay="1" data-field="about.lead">{lead}</p>
            )}
            {paraRows.map((row, k: number) => (
              <p
                class="pcw-ab-p reveal"
                data-delay={String((k % 3) + 1)}
                data-field={`about.paragraphs.${row.i}`}
                set:html={row.text}
              ></p>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
        {hasPhoto && (
          /* The RIGHT cell, as the design draws it. .plate already gives the
             --well ground, the --r (zero) corner and the overflow clip; only the
             design's 440px is added. */
          <div class="plate pcw-ab-plate reveal" data-delay="1">
            <div
              class="ph"
              data-image-field="about.image"
              role={photoName ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={photoName || undefined}
              aria-hidden={photoName ? undefined : 'true'}
              style={`background-image:url('${imageCss}');`}
            ></div>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
      )}
      {specRows.length > 0 && (
        <div class="pcw-ab-specs">
          {specRows.map((row) => (
            /* Each cell owns its own two hairlines, which is what makes a
               continuous ruled ledger rather than a stack of boxes — and is why
               a dropped row takes its rules with it. Both spans are guarded
               separately, so a half-filled row prints one cell, not one cell
               plus a gap. */
            <div class="pcw-ab-spec reveal">
              {row.label && (
                <span class="mono pcw-ab-sl" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.label`}>{row.label}</span>
              )}
              {row.value && (
                <span class="pcw-ab-sv" data-field={`about.specs.${row.i}.value`}>{row.value}</span>
              )}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* THE CONTENT CELL IS UNPADDED ON PURPOSE — see DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED in
     the frontmatter. The padding in this section belongs to the copy half and
     to each ledger cell, so that every hairline can run from one edge of the
     sheet to the other. min-width:0 because it is a grid child of .sec and its
     ledger must be allowed to shrink rather than widen the document. */
  .pcw-ab-body { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE SPLIT, verbatim: two EQUAL tracks and no gap. The division between them
     is a border on the copy cell, which is this template's rule everywhere —
     grids are drawn with rules, not with space. */
  .pcw-ab-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  .pcw-ab-split--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* The copy half. The design writes `padding:56px 40px`; those two numbers are
     the CAPS of the clamps below, so the design's value is what a desktop
     renders and the only movement is downward as the viewport narrows (R8).
     The border-right is the design's interior hairline — the quiet --line,
     never the --rule-strong that closes the whole section. */
  .pcw-ab-copy {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: clamp(28px, 4vw, 56px) clamp(20px, 3vw, 40px);
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* A half-empty sheet gets no hairline down its middle: with no photograph
     there is nothing on the other side of that rule to divide from. */
  .pcw-ab-split--solo .pcw-ab-copy { border-right: 0; }

  /* THE HEADLINE. .h2 supplies the family, the 800 weight, the -.03em, the
     uppercase and the --ink; the three metrics the design states for THIS
     section are set here because they are not .h2's — a smaller clamp, a
     line-height of exactly 1, and the .34em gap under it. The size is declared
     ONCE with no query of its own, which is what keeps the ladder monotonic
     across 760 and 520 (R8, traced in the frontmatter). max-width is left to the
     wrapper on purpose: .h2's 20ch cap draws roughly the short measure the
     design breaks its own headline to, and PageBN's 760 pass releases it for
     one column — forking it here would put this heading out of step with the
     other fourteen sections. The rust second voice is PageBN's `:where(.h2 em)`
     global rule; a scoped rule could not reach markup made by set:html (R3). */
  .pcw-ab-copy .pcw-ab-h {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0 0 .34em;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* THE PARAGRAPH. Deliberately NOT the wrapper's .lede: .lede is --ink-soft at
     clamp(15px,1.4vw,16.5px)/1.62 with a 52ch cap, and this design's about copy
     is a flat 16.5px at 1.82 in the darker --ink-2. .lede states SEVEN
     declarations and this copy disagrees with five of them — colour, size,
     leading, measure and margin; only its text-transform and letter-spacing
     would survive untouched. Overriding five of seven would be borrowing a
     primitive in order to disagree with it, so the design's own values are
     stated on a class of this section's own.
     PageBN's `html[data-page="BN"] :where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1) — the
     attribute plus the `html` element, :where() adding nothing. Astro's
     default 'attribute' strategy scopes EVERY compound of a scoped selector,
     so the rule below compiles with two classes AND two attributes — (0,4,0),
     safely above it whatever order the sheets land in.
     The 66ch cap is the one value the design does not write. In the two-column
     state the copy COLUMN is normally the narrower constraint, so on an ordinary
     desktop the cap does nothing and the design renders as drawn; it begins to
     bite only on a very wide sheet. Where it earns its place is the STACKED and
     SOLO states — solo being the default first build, since
     derive-content-defaults seeds no about.image — because there a paragraph
     would otherwise run the full width of the sheet, which is not a measure
     anyone reads. No exact crossover width is claimed: a ch is Archivo's own "0"
     advance, which is not a number this file can compute. */
  .pcw-ab-copy .pcw-ab-p {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.82;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design gives its LAST paragraph `margin:0`; the cell's own bottom
     padding is the gap above the ledger. */
  .pcw-ab-copy .pcw-ab-p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The photograph's own height. .plate knows the --well ground, the zero corner
     and the clip; the design's 440px floor is the one thing it does not — and it
     is a FLOOR, not a height, so the photograph grows with the copy beside it
     exactly as the design's stretched cell does.
     THE PARENT QUALIFIER IS INSURANCE, not the thing that makes this work — an
     earlier note here had the arithmetic the wrong way round. PageBN floors
     every .plate at 180px through `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.plate)`, which
     is (0,1,1). astro.config.mjs sets no scopedStyleStrategy, so Astro's default
     'attribute' applies and a bare `.pcw-ab-plate` compiles to
     `.pcw-ab-plate[data-astro-cid-…]` — (0,2,0), already ABOVE the wrapper, so
     the design's 440px would render either way. That strategy scopes EVERY
     compound, so qualifying by the split takes it to (0,4,0) — two classes and
     two attributes. The qualifier stays because it is what survives a switch to
     `scopedStyleStrategy: 'where'`, under which the bare class would fall to
